  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nA daily chance of showers and thunderstorms is in the forecast \ntoday through Wednesday. With this, a few thunderstorms may become\nstrong to severe over southern Montana and northern Wyoming, \ngreatest risk Tuesday, with strong winds, hail, and heavy rain \nbeing the main threats. The forecast trends drier Thursday into \nthe weekend as hot temperatures build back in over the region. \n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop and move northeast through the area late this afternoon \nthrough tonight as a shortwave trough approaches the region from \nthe southwest. With modest instability and shear, a few \nthunderstorms may become strong to severe late this afternoon and \nevening over south-eastern Montana and north-central/eastern \nWyoming (Marginal Risk, 1/5). The main hazards are strong winds \nand heavy rain, though isolated hail cannot be ruled out, \nespecially with discrete thunderstorms that develop initially off \nthe mountains. Over south- central Montana, the isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms that are forecast this \nafternoon and evening are not expected to become strong to severe \ndue to limited instability as a result of cooler temperatures and \ncloud cover. With that said, brief heavy rain, small hail, and \ngusty winds could still occur. While the precipitation will come \nto an end tonight over south- central Montana, a developing low \nlevel jet will keep isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms in the forecast tonight into Tuesday morning over \neastern Montana. While severe thunderstorms are not expected \nduring this time, rainfall from showers and thunderstorms may push\nstrong south- easterly wind gusts in the 30s to near 50 mph down \nto the surface at times.\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are forecast \nagain Tuesday afternoon and evening as the previously mentioned \nshortwave trough moves through the area. While limited shear may \nresult in shorter-lived thunderstorms over south-central Montana, \nincreased shear and instability over south-eastern Montana and \nnorth-central/eastern Wyoming could result in longer-lived \nthunderstorms. With this, a few thunderstorms could become strong \nto severe over south- eastern Montana and north- central/eastern \nWyoming (mostly a Marginal Risk, 1/5 with a small area of Slight \nRisk, 2/5 over far south-eastern Montana). The main hazards are \nstrong winds, hail, and heavy rain. With precipitable water values\naround 150 to 200 percent of normal, there is also a Marginal \nRisk (1/4) of Excessive Rainfall causing flash flooding Tuesday. \n\nAnother shortwave trough looks to bring a continued chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms Wednesday across the area and Thursday \nover eastern Montana. With this, there is a low risk of severe \nthunderstorms on Wednesday. \n\nFriday into the weekend, dry conditions return as temperatures \nwarm into the 90s to lower 100s, warmest Saturday and Sunday. \nSaturday night and Sunday night, overnight low temperatures look \nto remain in the 60s to lower 70s across the plains. This, \ncombined with the hot daytime temperatures, will increase the \nrisk of heat impacts. LA",
